aid workers and emergency responders  know what to do when hundreds of  thousands of people are in need the  problem in Mozambique is getting the aid  to the people who need it  Sky News boarded a soviet-era helicopter  chartered by the World Food Program it's  Ukrainian crew taking family size tents  and survival meals to a town called nama  tanda but first we had to cross the  giant Inland Sea the product of cyclone  EDI and five days of torrential rain  the weather wasn't the only complication  we were traveling to a remote community  and the pilots couldn't find it the  floodwaters had swallowed up the  landmarks on their Maps  with fuel running low the crew had  little choice they decided to return to  base difficult isn't it okay equipped  with a new set of coordinates we flew  once more for nama tender and this time  we found it a handful of residents  signaling from the landing zone by the  time we touched down thousands had  arrived to greet us this the first aid  delivery to reach the town we're in a  really bad situation we don't have  anything to cover us and everything is  in water including our food our  helicopter it was swiftly unloaded in  front of a large and expectant audience  but our cargo was grossly inadequate  more than ten thousand families in this  district have lost their homes and  they're disappointed there's no hope for  them the man who runs this district told  me he was feeling the pressure he simply  can't meet the people's basic needs is  this enough or do you need more help we  need more because we're not talking only  about these residents everybody into  this tree there are people in other  areas we still can't bring them food by  car support of sheikh waheed they've  unloaded everything in about 10 minutes  not very long and we've got to go these  guys have got another mission to do and  we'll leave these people behind  I don't think they want us to go this is  not the best way to deliver aid lorries  carry much more cargo but in flood-hit  mozambique it's the best way and the  only way to keep people alive John  sparks Sky News Mozambique
 

